**The rubric I used**

There is no task sheet for this one — it was written before the engine
existed, so there was nothing to grade it against. I graded the file on the
three things that have always mattered here:

- **40 — it runs.** It compiles or interprets without an error.
- **30 — it is right.** The output is what it claims to produce.
- **30 — it explains itself.** Comments say *why*, not *what*.

**40 + 30 + 0 = 70%**

**What is solid**

The shape is right: ask how many, read that many, keep a running total. Given 3 and the numbers 10, 20 and 30 it prints `Sum=60`, which is right. It compiles clean.

**What is not**

Nothing is checked. `scanf("%d", &n)` decides how many times the loop runs, and if that read failed the loop runs against a meaningless count — and each `scanf("%d", &num)` inside the loop can fail too, adding an untouched number to the total instead of the one the user meant. There is no comment, and the two things worth saying out loud here are *why the total starts at zero* and *why the count is read before the numbers*.

**The one thing to fix first**

Check the count, then check each number:

```c
if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n < 0) {
    printf("How many numbers?\n");
    return 1;
}
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    if (scanf("%d", &num) != 1) return 1;
    sum += num;
}
```
